{title: Good King Wenceslas}
{artist: John Mason Neale}
{genre: Christmas}
{style: Carol}
{flow: Opening}
{key: G}
{tempo: 100}
{time: 4/4}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[G]Good King Wenceslas looked [D]out
On the [G]feast of Stephen
When the [G]snow lay round a[D]bout
[C]Deep and crisp and [G]even
[G]Brightly shone the [D]moon that night
Though the [G]frost was cruel
When a [G]poor man came in [D]sight
[C]Gath'ring winter [G]fuel
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[G]Hither, page, and stand by [D]me
If thou [G]know'st it, telling
[G]Yonder peasant, who is [D]he
[C]Where and what his [G]dwelling
[G]Sire, he lives a good league [D]hence
Under[G]neath the mountain
[G]Right against the forest [D]fence
By Saint [C]Agnes' [G]fountain
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[G]Bring me flesh and bring me [D]wine
Bring me [G]pine logs hither
[G]Thou and I will see him [D]dine
[C]When we bear them [G]thither
[G]Page and monarch forth they [D]went
Forth they [G]went together
[G]Through the rude wind's wild la[D]ment
And the [C]bitter [G]weather
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
[G]Sire, the night is darker [D]now
And the [G]wind blows stronger
[G]Fails my heart, I know not [D]how
[C]I can go no [G]longer
[G]Mark my footsteps, good my [D]page
Tread thou [G]in them boldly
[G]Thou shalt find the winter's [D]rage
Freeze thy [C]blood less [G]coldly
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 5}
[G]In his master's steps he [D]trod
Where the [G]snow lay dinted
[G]Heat was in the very [D]sod
[C]Which the saint had [G]printed
[G]Therefore, Christian men, be [D]sure
Wealth or [G]rank possessing
[G]Ye who now will bless the [D]poor
Shall your[C]selves find [G]blessing
{end_of_verse}
